Day 265/300: Regular Expression Matching

Problem Statement #10

Problem Statement Explanation:

Problem Statement
Implement regular expression matching with support for '.' and '*'.

'.' Matches any single character.
'*' Matches zero or more of the preceding element.
The matching should cover the entire input string (not partial).

Example:

Input: s = "aa", p = "a*"
Output: true (The '*' means zero or more of the preceding element, 'a'. In this case, it matches two 'a's).

Solution Approach:

This is a classic "Two-String DP" problem. We create a 2D table dp[i][j] representing whether the first i characters of string s match the first j characters of pattern p.

The Core Logic:
If characters match (or pattern is '.'):

The result is whatever the result was for the previous characters: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1].

If the pattern is `'*'’:

Case 0 (Zero occurrences): We ignore the * and the character before it. We look back two steps in the pattern: dp[i][j] = dp[i][j-2].
Case 1 (One or more occurrences): If the character before * matches the current character in s, we "consume" one character from s and keep the * active: dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j].

Here's the code:

class Solution {
    public boolean isMatch(String s, String p) {
        int m = s.length();
        int n = p.length();
        boolean[][] dp = new boolean[m + 1][n + 1];

        // Base case: Empty string and empty pattern match
        dp[0][0] = true;

        // Handle patterns like a*, a*b*, etc. matching an empty string
        for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
            if (p.charAt(j - 1) == '*') {
                dp[0][j] = dp[0][j - 2];
            }
        }

        for (int i = 1; i <= m; i++) {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++) {
                char sc = s.charAt(i - 1);
                char pc = p.charAt(j - 1);

                if (pc == sc || pc == '.') {
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1];
                } else if (pc == '*') {
                    // Two choices: 
                    // 1. Treat '*' as matching 0 of the preceding character
                    dp[i][j] = dp[i][j - 2];
                    
                    // 2. If preceding char matches, treat '*' as matching 1+ characters
                    char prevPc = p.charAt(j - 2);
                    if (prevPc == sc || prevPc == '.') {
                        dp[i][j] = dp[i][j] || dp[i - 1][j];
                    }
                }
            }
        }

        return dp[m][n];
    }
}

Solution Approach:

Time Complexity: O(M×N)
Space Complexity	O(M×N)	
